.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:12:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:13:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:14:# Decide `feat:` vs `docs:` commit type for behavior-introducing SKILL.md edits
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:15:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:16:## What
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:18:When committing a change to a `SKILL.md` file (or any `.md` workflow-specification file) that introduces net-new workflow behavior rather than correcting existing prose, decide the commit type by these steps:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:20:- [ ] Does the change introduce net-new workflow behavior (a new procedure step, a new decision branch, a new state-machine element)?
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:21:  - If yes: `feat:` is defensible (workflow-as-spec — the spec IS the behavior change).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:22:  - If no (prose correction, clarification, rewording): use `docs:`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:23:- [ ] Has the project ratified a rule for this disambiguation? Check `.gobbi/projects/gobbi/rules/` for a commit-type rule for SKILL.md edits.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:24:- [ ] If no rule exists: surface the question to the user before committing, so the decision can be captured as a project rule.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:25:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:26:## Why
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:27:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:28:The commit that shipped the worktree-create row (`feat(orchestration): add Configuration Step 1 worktree creation`) was a `.md`-only diff that nonetheless introduced workflow behavior (the worktree-creation procedure). `git/conventions.md` says `feat:` is for source features and `docs:` for documentation-only changes, but it gives no tie-breaker for SKILL.md edits that are documentation in form yet behavior in effect. Without a ratified rule, each such commit re-litigates the type choice.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:29:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:30:## Verification
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:31:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:32:The chosen commit type matches the decision tree above, and — once a project rule is ratified — a corresponding commit-type rule exists under `.gobbi/projects/gobbi/rules/`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:33:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:34:## Status notes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:35:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/checklists/skill-md-commit-type-feat-vs-docs.md:36:Pending user ratification as a standing project rule. The originating commit was not changed retroactively. Reference: `git/conventions.md` § Commit Type registry. Surfaced by a Claude consistency-evaluation finding during the worktree-create session-architecture work (session `1b26cf20`).
