.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:10:tags: [git-workflow, session-commits, memorization, cadence]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:11:design-id: D-4
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:12:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:13:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:14:# Per-iteration session-memory commit cadence (D-4)
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:15:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:16:## Context
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:18:Under worktree-first, the entire session directory lives in the worktree, and the worktree is removed at Wrap-up. Removal discards any uncommitted state, so session memory written during the session is lost on a mid-session abort unless it has been committed first. The open question was *when* to commit session memory to the worktree branch: once at Wrap-up, or at every iteration.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:20:## Decision
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:21:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:22:Each loop's MEMORIZATION step ends with a worktree-branch commit:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:23:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:24:```
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:25:git -C "$worktreePath" add <session-memory-deltas>
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:26:git -C "$worktreePath" commit -m "chore(session): record <loop> iter{n} memory"
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:27:```
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:28:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:29:Subject format: `chore(session): record <loop> iter{n} memory` (e.g., `chore(session): record ideation iter3 memory`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:30:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:31:**Cadence**: per-iteration (matches existing MEMORIZATION runs — at most `maxIterations` commits per loop × 5 loops = 15 commits per session).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:32:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:33:## Rationale
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:34:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:35:Worktree removal at Wrap-up discards uncommitted state; committed changes survive worktree removal. Per-iteration cadence (rather than once-at-Wrap-up) ensures session memory survives mid-session abort — all data up to the last completed iteration is durable.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:36:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:37:## Alternatives considered
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:38:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:39:- **Commit once at Wrap-up** — rejected: session-memory data is lost on a mid-session abort that happens before Wrap-up runs. Per-iteration cadence makes every completed iteration's data durable as it lands.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:40:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:41:## Consequences
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:42:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:43:This cadence applies to all 5 workflow loop docs (`ideation.md`, `preparation.md`, `planning.md`, `execution.md`, `wrap-up.md`); see `design/workflow-phase-doc-set-for-per-iter-cadence.md` for the explicit file enumeration. Verification: in a multi-iteration loop, `git log --oneline` inside the worktree shows subjects matching `^chore\(session\): record .* iter[0-9]+ memory$`. The accumulated commits add a bounded storage cost to develop's history (see `decisions/2026-05-24-session-commit-storage-bounds.md`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:44:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/per-iteration-session-commit-cadence.md:45:## Related
