.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:16:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:18:# Worktree scope-by-module + commit-at-session-boundaries
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:20:## Insight
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:21:Community-validated practice for parallel Claude Code worktrees codifies three rules: (1) scope worktrees by module rather than task — tasks in the same module share a worktree sequentially, tasks across modules go to parallel worktrees; (2) rebase, not merge, between worktrees so `git log` stays readable for the agent; (3) commit at session boundaries — uncommitted changes are the only state that does not survive a session reset, so keep that state tiny. Two-to-three parallel sessions is the sustainable ceiling.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:22:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:23:## Related
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:24:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:25:- `claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md` — the Claude Code worktree primitive this scoping discipline applies to.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:26:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:27:## Why it applies
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:28:The gobbi worktree-first design locks scope at session granularity (one worktree per session, not per module). This reference is the counter-position to that choice — it documents the trade-offs the user is implicitly accepting (more worktrees per feature, higher cognitive load if parallelism grows). Rule (3) "uncommitted changes are the only state that does not survive a session reset" is directly relevant to the session-memory-survival design question: the project's analogue is "session memory directory contents are the only state that does not survive worktree removal" → if session memory lives in the worktree, it must be committed (or mirrored) before worktree removal.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:29:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:30:## Source
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:31:- https://www.mindstudio.ai/blog/parallel-agentic-development-claude-code-worktrees
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:32:- MindStudio, 2026, accessed 2026-05-23
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:33:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:34:## Excerpt
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:35:> "Rule 1: Scope worktrees by module, not by task. Tasks in the same module share a worktree (sequentially)… Rule 3: Commit at session boundaries. Every time a session ends — not every time Claude stops — you commit. Uncommitted changes are the only state that does not survive a session reset. Make that state tiny."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:36:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md:37:## Usage history
