.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:10:tags: [worktree, jujutsu, prior-art, session-isolation, sub-agent]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:11:title: Jujutsu jj workspace — revision-anchored isolation primitive for sub-agent dev
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:12:source: https://www.joshualyman.com/2026/02/demystifying-jujutsu-jj-workspaces/
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:13:accessed: 2026-05-23
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:14:ref_type: blog
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:15:related: [claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:16:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:18:# Jujutsu jj workspace as session-isolation prior art
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:20:## Insight
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:21:Jujutsu workspaces are filesystem-independent working copies that share the same `.jj` repo but each have their own working copy — like git worktrees, but anchored to a revision rather than a branch. Critically, the community frames `jj workspace` as "an ideal unit of isolation for sub-agent driven development: spin up one workspace per parallel task, let each agent iterate in its own directory, and merge back through jj's usual revision graph instead of branch-and-merge ceremony." This is a stronger architectural claim than git worktrees alone: the workspace is treated as the *primary* isolation unit, not an optional add-on. Plugin ecosystems already exist (`jj-worktree`, `claude-jj-worktree`) that intercept `git worktree` calls and convert them to `jj workspace` operations.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:22:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:23:## Related
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:24:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:25:- `claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md` — the git-worktree primitive this jj-workspace prior art generalizes.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:26:- `claude-jj-worktree-shim-pattern.md` — the shim plugins that route git-worktree calls to these jj workspaces.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:27:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:28:## Why it applies
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:29:The gobbi worktree-first design declares one worktree per session (uniform for every session). The closest prior art outside the git ecosystem is jj workspaces — and the framing "one workspace per parallel task" is the exact framing the worktree-first design generalizes. Two practical consequences: (a) the design direction is community-validated as the right primitive for sub-agent isolation, not a project-local idiosyncrasy; (b) future evolution to jj is forward-compatible if the spec keeps the abstraction at "isolated working surface per session" rather than "git worktree specifically." The jj framing also suggests an answer to the session-memory-survival design question: the underlying repo (`.jj` / `.git`) is the durable layer — if session memory commits to a branch, it lives in the repo, not the working copy, so it survives worktree removal automatically.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:30:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:31:## Source
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:32:- https://www.joshualyman.com/2026/02/demystifying-jujutsu-jj-workspaces/
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:33:- "Demystifying Jujutsu (jj) Workspaces" — Joshua Lyman, accessed 2026-05-23
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:34:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:35:## Excerpt
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:36:> "Unlike git worktrees, they're not tied to a branch — they're tied to a revision… This makes workspaces an ideal unit of isolation for sub-agent driven development: spin up one workspace per parallel task, let each agent iterate in its own directory, and merge back through jj's usual revision graph instead of branch-and-merge ceremony."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md:37:
