.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:16:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:18:# Claude Code official worktree-isolation pattern
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:20:## Insight
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:21:Claude Code natively supports per-session worktree isolation: the `-w` flag spins each session in its own working directory + branch, and subagents can carry `isolation: worktree` in their frontmatter so they are placed in a worktree by default. Edits in one session never touch files in another — worktrees are the official isolation primitive the runtime offers, not a project-side hack.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:22:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:23:## Related
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:24:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:25:- `jj-workspace-isolation-revision-not-branch.md` — the cross-VCS (Jujutsu) counterpart of this isolation primitive.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:26:- `worktree-scope-by-module-not-task.md` — community worktree-scoping discipline that builds on this Claude Code primitive.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:27:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:28:## Why it applies
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:29:The gobbi worktree-first design aligns with the runtime-recommended isolation model. If gobbi declares every session must bootstrap a worktree at Configuration Step 1, it is harmonizing with the runtime's own pattern rather than inventing a project-local discipline. Importantly, the docs also call out a tightly-relevant constraint for the session-memory-survival design question: "Git worktrees isolate your files, but they don't isolate your database, environment variables, or running services" — i.e., the runtime treats worktree isolation as filesystem-only; cross-session state (e.g., session memory) must be addressed separately.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:30:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:31:## Source
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:32:- https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:33:- Claude Code official docs, accessed 2026-05-23
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:34:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:35:## Excerpt
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:36:> "Running each Claude Code session in its own worktree means edits in one session never touch files in another, so you can have Claude building a feature in one terminal while fixing a bug in a second… Git worktrees isolate your files, but they don't isolate your database, environment variables, or running services."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:37:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/references/claude-code-worktree-isolation-pattern.md:38:## Usage history
